The  device  allows  to create, control, and delete Lua states in
the kernel through an interface.  Moreover, can be used  to  load
Lua scripts into a Lua state and to assign modules to an existing
state,  i.e.  perform  the  equivalent of the Lua command is also
used to retrieve information about currently active  Lua  states.
Lua  modules are used to provide functionality to Lua scripts not
available in the language itself,  e.g.  to  access  core  kernel
functionality  like printing text on the console.  Unlike in user
space Lua, where Lua modules are files in the filesystem, modules
must be provided to in the form of loadable kernel  modules  that
register  their  functionality  with Modules are loaded using the
Lua command; whether this command is available  or  not  is  con‐
trolled  by a variable.  by default tries to load a kernel module
named when it encounters the Lua command The operation of can  be
controlled  by  means  of the following variables: When set to 1,
tries to autoload kernel modules.  The default value is 1.   When
set  to 1, loading of Lua bytecode is allowed.  The default value
is 0.  When set to a value > 0, limits the number of instructions
executed to this number.  The default value is 0.  When set to 1,
enables the command in Lua.  The default value is 1.  When set to
a value > 0, verbosity is increased.  The  default  value  is  0.
The  following structures and constants are defined in the header
file: Returns information about the states in the structure: #de‐
fine MAX_LUA_NAME           16 #define MAX_LUA_DESC            64

struct   lua_state_info   {           char    name[MAX_LUA_NAME];
        char    desc[MAX_LUA_DESC];         bool    user; };

struct lua_info {         int num_states;         /* total number
of Lua states */         struct lua_state_info *states; }; Create
a new named Lua state with name and description in the structure:
struct     lua_create    {            char    name[MAX_LUA_NAME];
        char    desc[MAX_LUA_DESC]; }; Destroy a named Lua state.
Perform the equivalent of the Lua command in a named state.   The
name  of the state and of the module name is passed in the struc‐
ture: #define LUA_MAX_MODNAME         32

struct   lua_require    {            char    state[MAX_LUA_NAME];
        char    module[LUA_MAX_MODNAME];  };  Load  Lua code from
the filesystem into a named Lua state.  The name of the state and
the path to the Lua code are  passed  in  the  structure:  struct
lua_load          {                  char    state[MAX_LUA_NAME];
        char    path[MAXPATHLEN]; };  The  path  element  of  the
structure  must contain at least one character.  Lua device file.
